2 mean 0.01144. Those are not in the provided sources but from previous interactions? They might be similar integrations. We don't have their code. But we can try to improve.\n\nWe need to produce a single candidate now. Let's design carefully.\n\nKey ideas:\n1. FastScanner with fread and direct buffer cursors (from A). Use `char* cur, end` and refill.\n2. Read n,s then read entire matrix into flat vector<int> of size n*n. Or maybe use int16? No, values can be large.\n3. Compute vertical prefix sums pref[(n+1)*n] as long long to avoid overflow. But that requires O(n^2) memory for pref (8 bytes each -> 512MB for n=8000). That's too much! Actually n*n = 64M; (n+1)*n ~ 64M * 8 = 512MB, plus matrix 256MB => 768MB. Might be too high. B uses only v(n) and pref(n+1) per thread, not full prefix sums. C's approach with full prefix sums is memory heavy but maybe tests don't use max N? But we should avoid.\n\nBetter: Use B's approach: read matrix into flat vector<int> a (256MB), then parallel over starting rows, each thread maintains column sums v(n) and pref(n+1). That uses only O(n^2 + n*threads) memory. Good.\n\nBut reading all input first is sequential; we can combine with fast scanner to speed up.\n\nAlternatively use A's ring buffer but parallelize? Could have each thread process a disjoint set of starting rows by having the main thread read and distribute rows? That would require storing s+1 rows per thread or something. Complex.\n\nGiven B measured 0.0128, C 0.0122, A 0.0113. The best single is A (sequential but very optimized). Our integration might combine A's scanner with B/C parallelization to get below 0.011.\n\nLet's design:\n\n- Use FastScanner from A: class with char buf[1<<20], cur/end pointers.\n- Read n,s.\n- Allocate vector<int> a(n*n) (flat).\n- Read all values into a row-major order using scanner, maybe unrolled by 4 as in A? But reading is sequential; we can just loop j and call nextInt. Unrolling might help but not necessary if scanner fast.\n- Then Ope