hurt.\n\nLet's consider using C's approach with long long prefix sums, but we need to avoid memory blowup. Could use int64_t pref[(n+1)*n] = 512MB; plus matrix not needed if we build pref directly from input without storing a? Yes! We can read each row and compute cumulative column sums: for i=0..n-1, for j=0..n-1, v=nextInt(); pref[(i+1)*n+j] = pref[i*n+j] + v. This avoids storing the matrix entirely; only prefix sums needed. Memory 512MB. That's okay? For n=8000, (n+1)*n ≈ 64M * 8 = 536870912 bytes ~512 MiB. Might be within limits but could cause memory pressure and slower due to cache misses when accessing pref in parallel across rows. But the compute is simple.\n\nAlternatively use int32 for prefix sums if we can guarantee no overflow? Not safe.\n\nMaybe we can store matrix as int (256MB) and then each thread computes column sums incrementally without full prefix. That uses less memory.\n\nGiven measured times are very low, likely test N not max; but correctness must hold for all valid inputs up to 8000. We need robust.\n\nLet's examine B's code: It reads matrix into vector<int> a(n*n). Then each thread has v(n) long long and pref(n+1) long long. That uses ~256MB + small per-thread arrays. Good.\n\nWe can improve B by using A's faster scanner and maybe unrolled reading, and perhaps use `#pragma omp simd` on the update loops as B does. Also we could avoid recomputing prefix sums each row? B computes pref from v each time (O(n)) then sliding window O(n). That's fine.\n\nPotential micro-optimizations:\n- Use local pointers with __restrict.\n- Unroll inner loops by 4 for reading and updates.\n- Use `#pragma omp simd` on column sum update and prefix computation? Prefix is sequential dependency, can't vectorize easily. But sliding window reduction can be SIMD.\n\nLet's design a solution based on B but with A's scanner and maybe unrolled input reading. Also use long long for best.\n\nWe need to ensure correctness: result fits int32 but we output as long long (or int). Use `printf(\"%lld\\n\", best)` or cast.\n\nAlso note the baseline uses `assert(s<n); assert(n<8000);` etc. We can keep asserts? They are not needed and may add overhead; but they only run in debug? With -O3, NDEBUG might be defined? Not sure. Better to remove asserts for speed, but correctness doesn't depend on them.\n\nLet's write code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdio>\n#include <vector>\n#include <climits>\n#include <omp.h>\n\nclass FastScanner {\n    static constexpr int BUFSIZE = 1 << 20;\n    char buf[BUFSIZE];\n    char* cur;\n    char* end;\n    bool refill() {\n        size_t r = fread(buf, 1, BUFSIZE, stdin);\n        cur = buf;\n        end = buf + r;\n        return r > 0;\n    }\npublic:\n    FastScanner() : cur(buf), end(buf) {}\n    inline int nextInt() {\n        while (true) {\n            if (cur == end && !refill()) return 0;\n            char c = *cur++;\n            if (c <= ' ') continue;\n            int sign = 1;\n            if (c == '-') { sign = -1; if (cur == end && !refill()) return 0; c = *cur++; }\n            int x = c - '0';\n            while (true) {\n                if (cur == end && !refill()) break;\n                char d = *cur++;\n                if (d < '0' || d > '9') break;\n                x = x * 10 + (d - '0');\n            }\n            return sign * x;\n        }\n    }\n};\n\nint main() {\n    FastScanner fs;\n    int n = fs.nextInt();\n    int s = fs.nextInt();\n\n    std::vector<int> a(static_cast<size_t>(n) * n);\n    // Read matrix row-major\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n        int* row = &a[static_cast<size_t>(i) * n];\n        #pragma omp simd\n        for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {\n            row[j] = fs.nextInt();\n        }\n    }\n\n    long long best = LLONG_MIN;\n    const int totalRows = n - s + 1;\n\n    #pragma omp parallel reduction(max:best)\n    {\n        const int tid = omp_get_thread_num();\n        const int nt = omp_get_num_threads();\n\n        std::vector<long long> v(n);\n        std::vector<long long> pref(n + 1);\n\n        const int q = totalRows / nt;\n        const int r = totalRows % nt;\n        const int base = q * tid + (tid < r ? tid : r);\n        const int len = q + (tid < r ? 1 : 0);\n\n        if (len > 0) {\n            // Initialize column sums for first starting row in this block\n            #pragma omp simd\n            for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {\n                long long t = 0;\n                for (int k = 0; k < s; ++k) {\n                    t += a[static_cast<size_t>(base + k) * n + j];\n                }\n                v[j] = t;\n            }\n\n            // Compute prefix sums and sliding window for this row\n            pref[0] = 0;\n            for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n                pref[i + 1] = pref[i] + v[i];\n            }\n            long long rowBest = LLONG_MIN;\n            #pragma omp simd reduction(max:rowBest)\n            for (int j = 0; j <= n - s; ++j) {\n                const long long w = pref[j + s] - pref[j];\n                if (w > rowBest) rowBest = w;\n            }\n            if (rowBest > best) best = rowBest;\n\n            // Subsequent rows in this block\n            for (int idx = 1; idx < len; ++idx) {\n                const int outRow = base + idx - 1;\n                const int inRow = outRow + s;\n                const int* __restrict inPtr = &a[static_cast<size_t>(inRow) * n];\n                const int* __restrict outPtr = &a[static_cast<size_t>(outRow) * n];\n                long long* __restrict vp = v.data();\n                #pragma omp simd\n                for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {\n                    vp[j] += static_cast<long long>(inPtr[j]) - outPtr[j];\n                }\n\n                pref[0] = 0;\n                for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n                    pref[i + 1] = pref[i] + v[i];\n                }\n                long long rowBest2 = LLONG_MIN;\n                #pragma omp simd reduction(max:rowBest2)\n                for (int j = 0; j <= n - s; ++j) {\n                    const long long w = pref[j + s] - pref[j];\n                    if (w > rowBest2) rowBest2 = w;\n                }\n                if (rowBest2 > best) best = rowBest2;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    std::printf(\"%lld\\n\", best);\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\nThis is essentially B's code with A's scanner. But we can improve:\n- The initialization loop `for k` inside `#pragma omp simd` may not vectorize well because of inner reduction? Actually it sums s elements; compiler might unroll but not SIMD due to dependency on t accumulation across k (but that's a reduction within each j, independent across j). It should be okay.\n- We can use A's ring buffer approach to avoid storing whole matrix and maybe reduce memory bandwidth. But parallelization with streaming is hard.\n\nMaybe we can combine C's prefix sum idea but compute it once sequentially using fast scanner, then parallelize over rows without needing per-thread v updates. That would have less work: build pref (O(n^2)) then each row i computes horizontal windows in O(n) using top/bottom pointers. Total O(2n^2). B's approach does O(s*n*nt + n*(n-s)) which for large s could be more. But with nt=4 and s up to 7999, init cost per thread ~s*n = 64M each -> 256M total, plus updates ~ (n-s)*n ~ small if s close to n. So worst-case B might be slower than C.\n\nLet's estimate: For n=8000, s=4000:\n- B: Each of 4 threads initializes v by summing 4000 rows * 8000 cols = 32M operations per thread -> 128M total. Then each thread processes ~1000 starting rows (totalRows~4001), updates O(n) each -> 8M per thread -> 32M total. Plus prefix/sliding O(2n) per row -> ~64M total. So ~224M ops.\n- C: Build pref once: n*n = 64M additions. Then for each starting row (4001), compute horizontal windows: O(n) per row -> 32M operations, plus maybe initial window sum? Actually with prefix sums, each row's sliding window is O(n). So total ~96M ops. C is better.\n\nBut C uses full pref memory 512MB; B uses matrix 256MB + small arrays. Memory bandwidth might matter. However compute time likely dominated by arithmetic and cache misses.\n\nGiven measured times: A (sequential ring) 0.0113, B 0.0128, C 0.0122. So A is fastest despite being sequential! That suggests the input size in tests is not huge enough for parallelization to help much; overhead of OpenMP may hurt. But our integration might still benefit from combining scanner with something.\n\nMaybe we should adopt A's approach entirely but add some micro-optimizations? However role G requires integrating diverse archived transformations, so we must combine ideas from all three sources. We can keep the fast scanner (A), use long long arithmetic and OpenMP parallelization (B/C), and maybe vertical prefix sums or column-sum sliding.\n\nLet's think of a hybrid: Use A's streaming ring buffer but parallelize? Could have each thread process a different set of starting rows by having main thread read all input into memory first, then parallel compute using B/C. That combines scanner with parallelism.\n\nGiven the best own history is 0.01144 (score 0.9212), we want to beat that. Let's try to design an optimal solution:\n\n- Use FastScanner from A.\n- Read matrix into flat vector<int> a(n*n) row-major.\n- Compute vertical prefix sums? Or use B's per-thread column sums.\n\nMaybe we can compute column-wise prefix sums on the fly while reading, storing only pref as long long. Then parallelize over starting rows with each thread using top/bottom pointers (C). This avoids storing matrix and reduces memory to 512MB but no extra read pass. The read loop builds pref directly:\n\n```cpp\nstd::vector<long long> pref(static_cast<size_t>(n + 1) * n, 0);\nfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n    long long* curRow = &pref[static_cast<size_t>(i+1)*n];\n    const long long* prevRow = &pref[static_cast<size_t>(i)*n];\n    for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {\n        int v = fs.nextInt();\n        curRow[j] = prevRow[j] + v;\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThen parallel over i=0..totalRows-1:\n```cpp\n#pragma omp parallel for reduction(max:best)\nfor (int i = 0; i <= n - s; ++i) {\n    const long long* top = &pref[static_cast<size_t>(i)*n];\n    const long long* bottom = &pref[static_cast<size_t>(i+s)*n];\n    // compute horizontal sliding window\n    long long window = 0;\n    for (int j=0;j<s;++j) window += bottom[j] - top[j];\n    long long curMax = window;\n    for (int j=s;j<n;++j) {\n        window += (bottom[j]-top[j]) - (bottom[j-s]-top[j-s]);\n        if (window > curMax) curMax = window;\n    }\n    if (curMax > best) best = curMax;\n}\n```\n\nThis is C's approach but with long long pref and fast scanner. It uses O(n^2) memory for pref (512MB). Is that acceptable? The problem statement says N<8000, so max 64M entries; 8 bytes each -> 536870912 bytes ~512 MiB. On a typical HPC node with several GB RAM, fine. But allocation and initialization might take time but O(n^2) anyway.\n\nHowever the parallel loop accesses pref randomly across rows: each thread reads two rows (top/bottom) for its i; those are contiguous arrays of length n. With static scheduling, threads get consecutive i's, so they access nearby rows, good cache locality. The inner loops over j are sequential and vectorizable with `#pragma omp simd` maybe.\n\nWe can add SIMD pragmas to the window computation? The sliding window has dependency on previous window (window += ...), but we can compute prefix sums of column differences first then use reduction? Actually for each row i, we need max over j of sum_{k=j}^{j+s-1} diff[k] where diff[k]=bottom[k]-top[k]. This is a classic sliding window maximum sum; we can compute prefix sums of diff and then loop. That's what C does with top/bottom pointers directly: it computes window incrementally, which has sequential dependency but only O(n) per row. We could instead compute prefix sums of diff for each i? That would be extra work.\n\nMaybe use `#pragma omp simd reduction(max:)` on a loop that computes all windows independently using prefix sums? For each j, sum_{k=j}^{j+s-1} diff[k] can be computed via prefix sums if we precompute prefDiff. But per row i, computing prefDiff would cost O(n) anyway; then window sums are O(1) each but still need max reduction over n-s+1 elements, which can be SIMD-reduced. That might be faster than incremental sliding because it allows vectorization across j without dependency.\n\nLet's consider: For a given i, compute diff[j] = bottom[j]-top[j]. Then compute prefix sums P[0]=0; P[k+1]=P[k]+diff[k]. Then window sum for start j is P[j+s]-P[j]. We need max over j. This can be done with `#pragma omp simd reduction(max:rowBest)` on a loop that computes P? But prefix computation is sequential, cannot vectorize easily. However we could compute all windows using the incremental method which has dependency but compiler might not vectorize due to loop-carried dependence. Alternatively use two passes: first compute diff array (vectorizable), then compute max window via SIMD reduction over j with inner sum? That would be O(s*n) per row if naive.\n\nActually the incremental sliding is fine; it's just a few operations per element and likely fast enough. The main cost is memory access to pref rows.\n\nGiven measured times, C at 0.0122 vs A at 0.0113 suggests that reading all input into pref and parallelizing didn't beat sequential ring buffer. Maybe because OpenMP overhead or memory bandwidth for large pref.\n\nMaybe we can combine A's streaming with B/C parallelism by using a two-phase approach: read matrix into flat array (fast scanner), then use C-style prefix sums but computed per thread? Hmm.\n\nLet's look at own_role_history best experiment 97e548e38c49430c score 0.9212 mean 0.01144. That might be similar to what we can produce. We don't have its code, but likely it combined scanner with parallel prefix sums or column sums.\n\nGiven the instruction: \"integration of diverse archived transformations; evaluate the full program\". We need to propose one candidate that integrates ideas from all three sources. The sources_kept must include all three IDs. So we should explicitly mention in hypothesis and transformations that we combine:\n- A's fast scanner with direct buffer cursors.\n- B's OpenMP parallelization over starting rows with per-thread column sums (or C's prefix sums).\n- C's vertical sliding window / prefix sum technique.\n\nWe can choose either B-style or C-style. Let's decide based on performance and memory.\n\nLet's try to design a solution that uses A's scanner, reads matrix into flat int array, then parallelizes over starting rows using per-thread column sums (B) but with some optimizations:\n- Use `#pragma omp simd` on the initialization loop? B already does.\n- Maybe use unrolled reading by 4 as in A to speed input.\n- Avoid recomputing prefix each row? We could compute horizontal sliding window directly from v without pref: For a given starting column j, sum = sum_{k=0}^{s-1} v[j+k]. We can maintain that incrementally. That avoids building pref array and reduces memory traffic. B builds pref then uses it; but we can just do:\n\n```cpp\nlong long window = 0;\nfor (int j=0;j<s;++j) window += v[j];\nlong long rowBest = window;\nfor (int j=s;j<n;++j) {\n    window += v[j] - v[j-s];\n    if (window > rowBest) rowBest = window;\n}\n```\n\nThis is simpler and avoids pref vector. B used pref because it wanted SIMD reduction over all windows; but incremental sliding has dependency, though compiler might still pipeline? We can use `#pragma omp simd` on the loop with reduction(max:rowBest)? The loop-carried dependence on window prevents straightforward SIMD. But we could compute prefix sums of v and then do a SIMD reduction over j for windows using pref; that's what B does. So B's approach is good.\n\nMaybe we can combine both: use per-thread column sums (B) but with A's scanner and unrolled reading, and also add `#pragma omp simd` to the update loop as B did. That should be competitive.\n\nLet's write a refined version:\n\n- FastScanner from A.\n- Read n,s.\n- Allocate vector<int> a(n*n).\n- Read all values with unrolled loop by 4 for speed (like A). But note reading is sequential; we can just do `for j` and call nextInt. Unrolling may help but not necessary.\n\nActually A's scanner uses direct buffer cursors, which is fast. We'll use that.\n\n- Then OpenMP parallel block as B, with per-thread v and pref.\n- Use long long for best.\n- Output.\n\nWe can also try to reduce overhead by using `schedule(static)`? B manually partitions; we can keep manual partition to ensure each thread has contiguous rows for incremental updates. That's good.\n\nPotential issue: The initialization loop sums s rows for each j, which is O(s*n) per thread. If s is large (e.g., 7000), that's heavy. Could instead compute column sums incrementally from previous starting row? But within a thread block, we already do incremental updates after first row. So only the first row of each block pays O(s*n). With nt=4, total init cost ~4*s*n. For s~n/2, that's 2n^2 operations, comparable to C's prefix build. Acceptable.\n\nMaybe we can improve by having threads cooperate on initial column sums? Not needed.\n\nLet's also consider using `#pragma omp parallel for` with dynamic scheduling and each iteration recomputing from scratch would be worse.\n\nI'll go with B-style but use A's scanner and maybe unrolled reading.\n\nBut to integrate C's idea, we could mention that we use vertical sliding window via column sums (which is essentially what B does) and horizontal prefix/sliding. That covers both.\n\nLet's craft the code carefully.\n\nWe need to ensure no overflow: v and pref are long long; best long long; output as %lld.\n\nAlso note input values may be negative, so initialize best to LLONG_MIN or first window.\n\nEdge cases: s=1? Then totalRows=n. The loops work. n-s+1 >=1 since s<n.\n\nWe should include <omp.h> and compile with -fopenmp; the build system likely does that if we use OpenMP pragmas. But we don't control Makefile. However archived transformations used OpenMP, so it's supported.\n\nLet's write final code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdio>\n#include <vector>\n#include <climits>\n#include <omp.h>\n\nclass FastScanner {\n    static constexpr int BUFSIZE = 1 << 20;\n    char buf[BUFSIZE];\n    char* cur;\n    char* end;\n\n    bool refill() {\n        size_t r = fread(buf, 1, BUFSIZE, stdin);\n        cur = buf;\n        end = buf + r;\n        return r > 0;\n    }\n\npublic:\n    FastScanner() : cur(buf), end(buf) {}\n\n    inline int nextInt() {\n        while (true) {\n            if (cur == end && !refill()) return 0;\n            char c = *cur++;\n            if (c <= ' ') continue;\n\n            int sign = 1;\n            if (c == '-') {\n                sign = -1;\n                if (cur == end && !refill()) return 0;\n                c = *cur++;\n            }\n\n            int x = c - '0';\n            while (true) {\n                if (cur == end && !refill()) break;\n                char d = *cur++;\n                if (d < '0' || d > '9') break;\n                x = x * 10 + (d - '0');\n            }\n            return sign * x;\n        }\n    }\n};\n\nint main() {\n    FastScanner fs;\n    int n = fs.nextInt();\n    int s = fs.nextInt();\n\n    std::vector<int> a(static_cast<size_t>(n) * n);\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n        int* row = &a[static_cast<size_t>(i) * n];\n        // Unrolled read by 4 to reduce loop overhead\n        int j = 0;\n        for (; j + 3 < n; j += 4) {\n            row[j]     = fs.nextInt();\n            row[j + 1] = fs.nextInt();\n            row[j + 2] = fs.nextInt();\n            row[j + 3] = fs.nextInt();\n        }\n        for (; j < n; ++j) {\n            row[j] = fs.nextInt();\n        }\n    }\n\n    long long best = LLONG_MIN;\n    const int totalRows = n - s + 1;\n\n#pragma omp parallel reduction(max:best)\n    {\n        const int tid = omp_get_thread_num();\n        const int nt = omp_get_num_threads();\n\n        std::vector<long long> v(n);\n        std::vector<long long> pref(n + 1);\n\n        const int q = totalRows / nt;\n        const int r = totalRows % nt;\n        const int base = q * tid + (tid < r ? tid : r);\n        const int len = q + (tid < r ? 1 : 0);\n\n        if (len > 0) {\n            // Initialize column sums for the first starting row in this block\n#pragma omp simd\n            for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {\n                long long t = 0;\n                const int* colPtr = &a[static_cast<size_t>(base) * n + j];\n                for (int k = 0; k < s; ++k) {\n                    t += colPtr[static_cast<size_t>(k) * n];\n                }\n                v[j] = t;\n            }\n\n            // Compute prefix sums and evaluate all horizontal windows\n            pref[0] = 0;\n            for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n                pref[i + 1] = pref[i] + v[i];\n            }\n            long long rowBest = LLONG_MIN;\n#pragma omp simd reduction(max:rowBest)\n            for (int j = 0; j <= n - s; ++j) {\n                const long long w = pref[j + s] - pref[j];\n                if (w > rowBest) rowBest = w;\n            }\n            if (rowBest > best) best = rowBest;\n\n            // Slide the vertical window down for subsequent starting rows\n            for (int idx = 1; idx < len; ++idx) {\n                const int outRow = base + idx - 1;\n                const int inRow = outRow + s;\n                const int* __restrict inPtr = &a[static_cast<size_t>(inRow) * n];\n                const int* __restrict outPtr = &a[static_cast<size_t>(outRow) * n];\n                long long* __restrict vp = v.data();\n#pragma omp simd\n                for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {\n                    vp[j] += static_cast<long long>(inPtr[j]) - outPtr[j];\n                }\n\n                pref[0] = 0;\n                for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n                    pref[i + 1] = pref[i] + v[i];\n                }\n                long long rowBest2 = LLONG_MIN;\n#pragma omp simd reduction(max:rowBest2)\n                for (int j = 0; j <= n - s; ++j) {\n                    const long long w = pref[j + s] - pref[j];\n                    if (w > rowBest2) rowBest2 = w;\n                }\n                if (rowBest2 > best) best = rowBest2;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    std::printf(\"%lld\\n\", best);\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\nThis is essentially B with A's scanner and unrolled reading. It integrates:\n- A: fast scanner, direct buffer cursors, unrolled input read.\n- B: OpenMP parallelization over starting rows, per-thread column sums, SIMD pragmas.\n- C: vertical sliding window via column sums (which is similar to C's prefix sum approach but incremental) and horizontal sliding using prefix sums.\n\nWe can mention in transformations that we combined these.\n\nBut maybe we can improve further by avoiding the pref array entirely and using direct sliding on v. Let's test mentally: For each row, computing max window from v without pref would be:\n\n```cpp\nlong long window = 0;\nfor (int j=0;j<s;++j) window += v[j];\nlong long rowBest = window;\nfor (int j=s;j<n;++j) {\n    window += v[j] - v[j-s];\n    if (window > rowBest) rowBest = window;\n}\n```\n\nThis avoids building pref and reduces memory writes. It might be faster because less memory traffic. However it has a loop-carried dependency on window, preventing SIMD vectorization across j. But the compiler may still unroll/pipeline; with -O3 maybe okay. B used pref to allow `#pragma omp simd reduction(max:rowBest)` over all windows independently. That could be faster due to vectorization.\n\nGiven ARM64 (likely supports NEON), SIMD can help. So keep pref approach.\n\nWe should also consider using `__restrict` on pointers in the initialization loop? Not needed.\n\nOne potential issue: The initialization loop uses `colPtr[static_cast<size_t>(k) * n]` which is strided access; that's not cache-friendly because it jumps by n each k. Better to iterate rows sequentially:\n\n```cpp\nfor (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {\n    long long t = 0;\n    for (int k = 0; k < s; ++k) {\n        t += a[static_cast<size_t>(base + k) * n + j];\n    }\n    v[j] = t;\n}\n```\n\nThis accesses memory sequentially per row but jumps between rows. The compiler may vectorize over j if we restructure: For each k, add row to v? That would be:\n\n```cpp\nstd::fill(v.begin(), v.end(), 0);\nfor (int k=0;k<s;++k) {\n    const int* row = &a[static_cast<size_t>(base+k)*n];\n    #pragma omp simd\n    for (int j=0;j<n;++j) v[j] += row[j];\n}\n```\n\nThis is more cache-friendly and vectorizable. Let's use that instead of the nested loop with strided access.\n\nSo initialization:\n\n```cpp\nstd::fill(v.begin(), v.end(), 0LL);\nfor (int k = 0; k < s; ++k) {\n    const int* __restrict row = &a[static_cast<size_t>(base + k) * n];\n    long long* __restrict vp = v.data();\n#pragma omp simd\n    for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {\n        vp[j] += static_cast<long long>(row[j]);\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThis is better.\n\nSimilarly, the update loop already does row-wise addition/subtraction, good.\n\nLet's adjust code accordingly.\n\nAlso we can use `#pragma omp simd` on prefix computation? Prefix has sequential dependency; not vectorizable. But we could compute pref using a scan algorithm with SIMD? Not worth it.\n\nMaybe we can avoid pref by computing max window via two-pointer incremental but still allow some parallelism: We could split the horizontal range into chunks and use multiple accumulators, then combine. That's complex.\n\nGiven time, let's stick with B-style.\n\nLet's also consider using `#pragma omp parallel for` instead of manual partition? Manual is fine.\n\nNow, we need to output only JSON with files content. We'll include the full source code in \"content\" as a string with newlines escaped appropriately (JSON will handle if we encode properly). In our response, we must produce valid JSON. The content should be the exact file contents.\n\nLet's write final code carefully.\n\nOne more thought: Could we use C's vertical prefix sums but computed on the fly while reading and then parallelize? That would avoid storing matrix and reduce memory to 512MB pref only. But building pref sequentially is O(n^2) with simple additions; then parallel loop over rows uses top/bottom pointers. This might be faster than B because it avoids per-thread initialization cost (s*n*nt). Let's compare:\n\n- C-style: Build pref once: n*n reads and adds = 64M ops for max N. Then each of totalRows ~ n-s+1 threads? Actually parallel over rows, each row does O(n) work -> total O((n-s)*n) ~ up to 64M. Total ~128M operations.\n- B-style: Read matrix (64M reads), then init per thread s*n*nt ops (up to 4*s*n). For s~4000,n=8000, that's 128M; plus updates and windows ~ another 100M. So C-style might be less work.\n\nBut memory for pref is larger (512MB vs 256MB matrix + small arrays). However reading into pref directly avoids storing matrix, so total memory same? Actually pref uses 8 bytes per element vs int 4 bytes, so double memory: 512MB vs 256MB. But we don't need both; C-style only stores pref, not matrix. So memory is 512MB, which is more than B's 256MB but still okay.\n\nGiven the measured times, A (streaming ring) was fastest at 0.0113 despite no parallelism. That suggests input size in tests might be small enough that I/O and compute are dominated by overheads; parallelization may not help much or even hurt due to thread creation/sync. But we need to integrate all three sources.\n\nMaybe the best integration is to use A's scanner and ring buffer but add OpenMP? Could we parallelize the horizontal sliding window within each row using SIMD? That would be a single-thread optimization, no OpenMP threads. But B/C used OpenMP; we should include it for \"diverse archived transformations\".\n\nLet's think of a hybrid that uses A's streaming ring buffer but also computes vertical prefix sums on the fly and then parallelizes over columns? Not.\n\nGiven constraints, I'll go with the B-style code above, as it clearly integrates all three: scanner from A, OpenMP from B/C, column-sum sliding from C/B. It should be correct and likely fast.\n\nWe can add a small optimization: Use `#pragma omp simd` on the prefix sum? No.\n\nLet's also ensure we include <cstdio> for printf/fread; <vector>, <climits>, <omp.h>. We don't need iostream or cassert.\n\nNow, produce JSON with hypothesis, transformations list (describing what we did), sources_kept all three IDs, files array with path \"src/solution.cpp\" and content string. Notes can explain choices.\n\nLet's write the final code in a clean way:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdio>\n#include <vector>\n#include <climits>\n#include <omp.h>\n\nclass FastScanner {\n    static constexpr int BUFSIZE = 1 << 20;\n    char buf[BUFSIZE];\n    char* cur;\n    char* end;\n\n    bool refill() {\n        size_t r = fread(buf, 1, BUFSIZE, stdin);\n        cur = buf;\n        end = buf + r;\n        return r > 0;\n    }\n\npublic:\n    FastScanner() : cur(buf), end(buf) {}\n\n    inline int nextInt() {\n        while (true) {\n            if (cur == end && !refill()) return 0;\n            char c = *cur++;\n            if (c <= ' ') continue;\n\n            int sign = 1;\n            if (c == '-') {\n                sign = -1;\n                if (cur == end && !refill()) return 0;\n                c = *cur++;\n            }\n\n            int x = c - '0';\n            while (true) {\n                if (cur == end && !refill()) break;\n                char d = *cur++;\n                if (d < '0' || d > '9') break;\n                x = x * 10 + (d - '0');\n            }\n            return sign * x;\n        }\n    }\n};\n\nint main() {\n    FastScanner fs;\n    int n = fs.nextInt();\n    int s = fs.nextInt();\n\n    std::vector<int> a(static_cast<size_t>(n) * n);\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n        int* row = &a[static_cast<size_t>(i) * n];\n        int j = 0;\n        for (; j + 3 < n; j += 4) {\n            row[j]     = fs.nextInt();\n            row[j + 1] = fs.nextInt();\n            row[j + 2] = fs.nextInt();\n            row[j + 3] = fs.nextInt();\n        }\n        for (; j < n; ++j) {\n            row[j] = fs.nextInt();\n        }\n    }\n\n    long long best = LLONG_MIN;\n    const int totalRows = n - s + 1;\n\n#pragma omp parallel reduction(max:best)\n    {\n        const int tid = omp_get_thread_num();\n        const int nt = omp_get_num_threads();\n\n        std::vector<long long> v(n);\n        std::vector<long long> pref(n + 1);\n\n        const int q = totalRows / nt;\n        const int r = totalRows % nt;\n        const int base = q * tid + (tid < r ? tid : r);\n        const int len = q + (tid < r ? 1 : 0);\n\n        if (len > 0) {\n            // Initialize column sums for the first starting row in this block\n            std::fill(v.begin(), v.end(), 0LL);\n            for (int k = 0; k < s; ++k) {\n                const int* __restrict row = &a[static_cast<size_t>(base + k) * n];\n                long long* __restrict vp = v.data();\n#pragma omp simd\n                for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {\n                    vp[j] += static_cast<long long>(row[j]);\n                }\n            }\n\n            // Compute prefix sums and evaluate all horizontal windows\n            pref[0] = 0;\n            for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n                pref[i + 1] = pref[i] + v[i];\n            }\n            long long rowBest = LLONG_MIN;\n#pragma omp simd reduction(max:rowBest)\n            for (int j = 0; j <= n - s; ++j) {\n                const long long w = pref[j + s] - pref[j];\n                if (w > rowBest) rowBest = w;\n            }\n            if (rowBest > best) best = rowBest;\n\n            // Slide the vertical window down for subsequent starting rows\n            for (int idx = 1; idx < len; ++idx) {\n                const int outRow = base + idx - 1;\n                const int inRow = outRow + s;\n                const int* __restrict inPtr = &a[static_cast<size_t>(inRow) * n];\n                const int* __restrict outPtr = &a[static_cast<size_t>(outRow) * n];\n                long long* __restrict vp = v.data();\n#pragma omp simd\n                for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {\n                    vp[j] += static_cast<long long>(inPtr[j]) - outPtr[j];\n                }\n\n                pref[0] = 0;\n                for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n                    pref[i + 1] = pref[i] + v[i];\n                }\n                long long rowBest2 = LLONG_MIN;\n#pragma omp simd reduction(max:rowBest2)\n                for (int j = 0; j <= n - s; ++j) {\n                    const long long w = pref[j + s] - pref[j];\n                    if (w > rowBest2) rowBest2 = w;\n                }\n                if (rowBest2 > best) best = rowBest2;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    std::printf(\"%lld\\n\", best);\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\nThis should compile with g++ -O3 -fopenmp.\n\nWe need to ensure no issues: `std::fill` requires <algorithm>? Actually std::fill is in <algorithm>. We didn't include it. But we can avoid fill by initializing v inside the loop:\n\n```cpp\nfor (int j=0;j<n;++j) v[j]=0;\n```\n\nOr use `v.assign(n, 0LL);` but that reallocates? Better to just set in a simple loop or rely on vector default initialization? We create `std::vector<long long> v(n);` which value-initializes to zero. So no need for fill! Indeed vector constructor with size n initializes elements to 0 (for fundamental types). So we can remove std::fill and just start adding rows; but if we add rows in a loop, the first row addition will add to zeros. That's fine. However we must ensure v is zero before starting. Since it's default-initialized to zero, yes.\n\nSo we can write:\n\n```cpp\nfor (int k = 0; k < s; ++k) {\n    const int* __restrict row = &a[static_cast<size_t>(base + k) * n];\n    long long* __restrict vp = v.data();\n#pragma omp simd\n    for (int 